	<section class="about-history container" id="history">
		<h2>How I got here</h2>
		<div>
			<p>
				I started teaching myself web development as a teenager <i>(during the era of PHP and ActionScript)</i> and took on my first paid projects when I was 18. I initially wanted to become a web designer who could also build and moved to Cologne to study Integrated Design at <a href="https://kisd.de/en/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="with-icon" style="--icon-src: url(../assets/img/kisd.webp);">Köln International School of Design</a> — but quickly realised I was much more interested in building things than designing them.
			</p>
			<p>
				I spent the next few years freelancing as a web developer before moving to Berlin in 2014 to join <a href="https://razorfish.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="with-icon" style="--icon-src: url(../assets/img/razorfish.webp);">Razorfish</a>. From there, I moved to <a href="https://mckinsey.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="with-icon" style="--icon-src: url(../assets/img/mck.png);">McKinsey & Company</a>, where my work gradually shifted towards larger software systems, solution architecture, and eventually engineering leadership.
			</p>
			<p>
				Over the years, I've worked in agencies, consulting, startups, and scaleups, and with some very large enterprises. The technologies and job titles have changed quite a bit, but I've mostly stayed close to the same kind of problems: understanding what needs to be built, figuring out how to build it well, and working with people to make it happen.
			</p>
			<p>
				These days I spend less time writing production code myself, but I still like staying as hands-on as possible and building things when I get the chance.
			</p>
			<p>
				For a more detailed and chronological version, there's my <a href="/resume" class="internal-link"><strong>resume</strong></a>.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

